---
title: Maps and Spatial Charts
description: Build choropleths, bubble maps, globes, routes, and vector fields for geographic or projected spatial questions.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spatial charts encode values in a coordinate system whose geometry already has\nmeaning. A geographic map uses a projection and feature boundaries. A vector\nfield uses position, direction, and magnitude. Neither should be treated as an\nordinary categorical chart with decorative shapes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-spatial-encoding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the spatial encoding"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does an aggregate differ across named regions?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choropleth"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where did individual events occur?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Projected point layer"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How do direction and magnitude vary over a sampled plane?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vector field"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does a path move through space?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Projected line with directional context"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must small regions be compared precisely by value?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorted bars or a table beside the map"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The application chooses the projection and spatial sampling policy. The\nopt-in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/geo"},{"type":"text","value":" entry uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-geo"},{"type":"text","value":" to turn GeoJSON into shared\nscene paths and interaction points. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-regional-aggregates","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare regional aggregates"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A choropleth joins one value to each named geographic feature and maps that\nvalue through a color scale."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"102-world-choropleth","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"108-country-choropleth","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"109-us-state-choropleth","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The join is part of data preparation. Match features through stable IDs, report\nunmatched records, and distinguish missing values from zero. Keep the color\ndomain and legend explicit so one filtered region cannot silently rescale the\nmeaning of every other fill."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Area draws attention. A large region can appear important even when its value\nis ordinary. Pair the map with a sorted table or bars when precise ranking is\npart of the task."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../guides/legends-and-color.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legends and Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers sequential,\ndiverging, and threshold choices."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prepare-atlas-boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prepare atlas boundaries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack accepts GeoJSON and does not bundle a political boundary dataset.\nConvert an application-owned TopoJSON atlas once, validate its feature count\nand IDs, then keep that geometry stable while values change."}]},{"type":"html","value":"<!-- docs-example: geo-atlas-ingest typecheck -->"},{"type":"code","value":"import { feature } from 'topojson-client'\nimport worldAtlas from 'world-atlas/countries-110m.json'\nimport type {\n  GeometryCollection,\n  Objects,\n  Topology,\n} from 'topojson-specification'\n\ninterface AtlasProperties {\n  name: string\n}\n\ntype WorldObjects = Objects<AtlasProperties> & {\n  countries: GeometryCollection<AtlasProperties>\n}\n\nconst topology = worldAtlas as unknown as Topology<WorldObjects>\nconst countries = feature<AtlasProperties>(topology, topology.objects.countries)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"world-atlas"},{"type":"text","value":" redistributes Natural Earth boundaries; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"us-atlas"},{"type":"text","value":" provides\nCensus-derived state geometry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"topojson-client.feature"},{"type":"text","value":" performs the standard\nconversion to GeoJSON consumed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":". These remain application\ndependencies, so neither their data nor the converter enters ordinary\nCartesian or geo-only consumer bundles."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"project-geojson-responsively","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Project GeoJSON responsively"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":" a projection factory and an explicit fit target. This\nself-contained example uses a small planar floor plan. The mark fits the\nprojection to the final plot bounds again whenever the chart resizes."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"floor-plan-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"floor-plan","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\\nimport { geoIdentity } from 'd3-geo'\\nimport { floorPlan } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    geoShape(floorPlan.features, {\\n      key: (feature) => feature.properties.id,\\n      projection: {\\n        type: geoIdentity,\\n        fit: floorPlan,\\n      },\\n      fill: '#dbeafe',\\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  margin: 12,\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"interface FloorPlanFeature {\\n  type: 'Feature'\\n  properties: { id: number; name: string }\\n  geometry: {\\n    type: 'Polygon'\\n    coordinates: [number, number][][]\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ninterface FloorPlan {\\n  type: 'FeatureCollection'\\n  features: FloorPlanFeature[]\\n}\\n\\nexport const floorPlan: FloorPlan = {\\n  type: 'FeatureCollection',\\n  features: [\\n    {\\n      type: 'Feature',\\n      properties: { id: 1, name: 'Studio' },\\n      geometry: {\\n        type: 'Polygon',\\n        coordinates: [\\n          [\\n            [0, 0],\\n            [48, 0],\\n            [48, 28],\\n            [0, 28],\\n            [0, 0],\\n          ],\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    },\\n    {\\n      type: 'Feature',\\n      properties: { id: 2, name: 'Office' },\\n      geometry: {\\n        type: 'Polygon',\\n        coordinates: [\\n          [\\n            [52, 0],\\n            [84, 0],\\n            [84, 28],\\n            [52, 28],\\n            [52, 0],\\n          ],\\n        ],\\n      },\\n    },\\n  ],\\n}\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { geoIdentity } from 'd3-geo'\nimport { floorPlan } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    geoShape(floorPlan.features, {\n      key: (feature) => feature.properties.id,\n      projection: {\n        type: geoIdentity,\n        fit: floorPlan,\n      },\n      fill: '#dbeafe',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  margin: 12,\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=floor-plan env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"interface FloorPlanFeature {\n  type: 'Feature'\n  properties: { id: number; name: string }\n  geometry: {\n    type: 'Polygon'\n    coordinates: [number, number][][]\n  }\n}\n\ninterface FloorPlan {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection'\n  features: FloorPlanFeature[]\n}\n\nexport const floorPlan: FloorPlan = {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection',\n  features: [\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 1, name: 'Studio' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [0, 0],\n            [48, 0],\n            [48, 28],\n            [0, 28],\n            [0, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 2, name: 'Office' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [52, 0],\n            [84, 0],\n            [84, 28],\n            [52, 28],\n            [52, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n  ],\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=floor-plan file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":" uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoPath"},{"type":"text","value":" for each feature and D3 centroids for focus. The\nfloor plan's source "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"properties.id"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies stable identity. Supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor"},{"type":"text","value":"\nonly when a geographic feature needs a different semantic longitude/latitude\nthan its spherical centroid. The complete option contract is in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/geo.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Geo Shape Mark"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/40-geojson-map/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the complete 121-polygon Westport House catalog example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"project-points-by-magnitude","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Project points by magnitude"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Point and MultiPoint features use D3 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoPath().pointRadius()"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"r"},{"type":"text","value":"\nchannel can carry pixels directly; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rScale"},{"type":"text","value":" maps a quantitative value first."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"103-bubble-map","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"change-the-projection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change the projection"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same GeoJSON can use any D3 projection factory. Sphere and graticule\ngeometry are ordinary "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":" layers."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"104-orthographic-globe","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"110-projection-gallery","height":"520"},"children":[]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"layer-routes-over-geography","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer routes over geography"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polygon, LineString, and Point features can share one responsive projection."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"105-route-map","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"show-direction-and-magnitude","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Show direction and magnitude"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A vector field places an arrow at each sampled position. Direction uses angle;\nmagnitude can use length, color, or both."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"42-vector-field","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose a sampling density that remains legible at the smallest container. More\narrows can obscure the flow instead of adding evidence. When pixel length\nencodes magnitude, define a bounded scale and disclose whether vectors were\nnormalized."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vector channel and anchor options are listed in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"projection-and-responsive-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Projection and responsive layout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A projection turns geographic coordinates into planar geometry. Fit it to the\nresolved plot bounds, not the viewport, and recompute when the chart container\nchanges. Preserve source longitude and latitude alongside projected\ncoordinates for tooltips and selection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a custom spatial shape must render beside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"geoShape"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep path generation deterministic and DOM-free."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit stable scene keys for each feature."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clip only when geometry should not extend beyond the plot."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit interaction points or application overlays only where they carry honest\nsemantic anchors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep projection and geo dependencies behind the spatial chart import."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"color-boundaries-and-missing-data","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color, boundaries, and missing data"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude with one direction."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a diverging scale only around a meaningful center."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use threshold colors when bins have defined policy meaning."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep boundary strokes visible in light and dark themes without overpowering\nfill."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give missing, suppressed, and out-of-domain features distinct treatment."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid a categorical palette for high-cardinality numeric values."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A legend is required whenever fill or vector color carries quantitative\nmeaning."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"accessibility-and-interaction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility and interaction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A map should not be the only route to critical regional values. Provide a table\nor list with the same feature names, values, units, and current selection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For interactive maps:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus and selection should resolve stable feature IDs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zoom and pan state belongs to the application."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset, keyboard, and touch paths should reach equivalent extents."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wheel capture should not trap page scrolling unexpectedly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips should name the region or sample, value, and unit."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defines\nthe controlled gesture loop."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validate feature joins and report unmatched IDs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State projection, aggregation unit, date range, and denominator."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep color domains comparable across views and revisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Test antimeridian, empty, missing, and extremely small features where\napplicable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bound projected point and vector counts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify labels and legends at narrow widths."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide a non-spatial exact-value path through\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-spatial-encoding","text":"Choose the spatial encoding","level":2},{"id":"compare-regional-aggregates","text":"Compare regional aggregates","level":2},{"id":"prepare-atlas-boundaries","text":"Prepare atlas boundaries","level":2},{"id":"project-geojson-responsively","text":"Project GeoJSON responsively","level":2},{"id":"project-points-by-magnitude","text":"Project points by magnitude","level":2},{"id":"change-the-projection","text":"Change the projection","level":2},{"id":"layer-routes-over-geography","text":"Layer routes over geography","level":2},{"id":"show-direction-and-magnitude","text":"Show direction and magnitude","level":2},{"id":"projection-and-responsive-layout","text":"Projection and responsive layout","level":2},{"id":"color-boundaries-and-missing-data","text":"Color, boundaries, and missing data","level":2},{"id":"accessibility-and-interaction","text":"Accessibility and interaction","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nSpatial charts encode values in a coordinate system whose geometry already has\nmeaning. A geographic map uses a projection and feature boundaries. A vector\nfield uses position, direction, and magnitude. Neither should be treated as an\nordinary categorical chart with decorative shapes.\n\n## Choose the spatial encoding\n\n| Reader question                                           | Start with                              |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| How does an aggregate differ across named regions?        | Choropleth                              |\n| Where did individual events occur?                        | Projected point layer                   |\n| How do direction and magnitude vary over a sampled plane? | Vector field                            |\n| How does a path move through space?                       | Projected line with directional context |\n| Must small regions be compared precisely by value?        | Sorted bars or a table beside the map   |\n\nThe application chooses the projection and spatial sampling policy. The\nopt-in `@tanstack/charts/geo` entry uses `d3-geo` to turn GeoJSON into shared\nscene paths and interaction points. See\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Compare regional aggregates\n\nA choropleth joins one value to each named geographic feature and maps that\nvalue through a color scale.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=102-world-choropleth height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=108-country-choropleth height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=109-us-state-choropleth height=480 -->\n\nThe join is part of data preparation. Match features through stable IDs, report\nunmatched records, and distinguish missing values from zero. Keep the color\ndomain and legend explicit so one filtered region cannot silently rescale the\nmeaning of every other fill.\n\nArea draws attention. A large region can appear important even when its value\nis ordinary. Pair the map with a sorted table or bars when precise ranking is\npart of the task.\n\n[Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers sequential,\ndiverging, and threshold choices.\n\n## Prepare atlas boundaries\n\nTanStack accepts GeoJSON and does not bundle a political boundary dataset.\nConvert an application-owned TopoJSON atlas once, validate its feature count\nand IDs, then keep that geometry stable while values change.\n\n<!-- docs-example: geo-atlas-ingest typecheck -->\n\n```ts\nimport { feature } from 'topojson-client'\nimport worldAtlas from 'world-atlas/countries-110m.json'\nimport type {\n  GeometryCollection,\n  Objects,\n  Topology,\n} from 'topojson-specification'\n\ninterface AtlasProperties {\n  name: string\n}\n\ntype WorldObjects = Objects<AtlasProperties> & {\n  countries: GeometryCollection<AtlasProperties>\n}\n\nconst topology = worldAtlas as unknown as Topology<WorldObjects>\nconst countries = feature<AtlasProperties>(topology, topology.objects.countries)\n```\n\n`world-atlas` redistributes Natural Earth boundaries; `us-atlas` provides\nCensus-derived state geometry. `topojson-client.feature` performs the standard\nconversion to GeoJSON consumed by `geoShape`. These remain application\ndependencies, so neither their data nor the converter enters ordinary\nCartesian or geo-only consumer bundles.\n\n## Project GeoJSON responsively\n\nGive `geoShape` a projection factory and an explicit fit target. This\nself-contained example uses a small planar floor plan. The mark fits the\nprojection to the final plot bounds again whenever the chart resizes.\n\n```ts group=floor-plan env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { geoIdentity } from 'd3-geo'\nimport { floorPlan } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    geoShape(floorPlan.features, {\n      key: (feature) => feature.properties.id,\n      projection: {\n        type: geoIdentity,\n        fit: floorPlan,\n      },\n      fill: '#dbeafe',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  margin: 12,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=floor-plan file=/src/data.ts collapsed\ninterface FloorPlanFeature {\n  type: 'Feature'\n  properties: { id: number; name: string }\n  geometry: {\n    type: 'Polygon'\n    coordinates: [number, number][][]\n  }\n}\n\ninterface FloorPlan {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection'\n  features: FloorPlanFeature[]\n}\n\nexport const floorPlan: FloorPlan = {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection',\n  features: [\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 1, name: 'Studio' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [0, 0],\n            [48, 0],\n            [48, 28],\n            [0, 28],\n            [0, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 2, name: 'Office' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [52, 0],\n            [84, 0],\n            [84, 28],\n            [52, 28],\n            [52, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n`geoShape` uses `geoPath` for each feature and D3 centroids for focus. The\nfloor plan's source `properties.id` supplies stable identity. Supply `anchor`\nonly when a geographic feature needs a different semantic longitude/latitude\nthan its spherical centroid. The complete option contract is in\n[Geo Shape Mark](../reference/marks/geo.md).\n\n[Open the complete 121-polygon Westport House catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/40-geojson-map/).\n\n## Project points by magnitude\n\nPoint and MultiPoint features use D3 `geoPath().pointRadius()`. The `r`\nchannel can carry pixels directly; `rScale` maps a quantitative value first.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=103-bubble-map height=480 -->\n\n## Change the projection\n\nThe same GeoJSON can use any D3 projection factory. Sphere and graticule\ngeometry are ordinary `geoShape` layers.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=104-orthographic-globe height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=110-projection-gallery height=520 -->\n\n## Layer routes over geography\n\nPolygon, LineString, and Point features can share one responsive projection.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=105-route-map height=480 -->\n\n## Show direction and magnitude\n\nA vector field places an arrow at each sampled position. Direction uses angle;\nmagnitude can use length, color, or both.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=42-vector-field height=480 -->\n\nChoose a sampling density that remains legible at the smallest container. More\narrows can obscure the flow instead of adding evidence. When pixel length\nencodes magnitude, define a bounded scale and disclose whether vectors were\nnormalized.\n\nVector channel and anchor options are listed in\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md).\n\n## Projection and responsive layout\n\nA projection turns geographic coordinates into planar geometry. Fit it to the\nresolved plot bounds, not the viewport, and recompute when the chart container\nchanges. Preserve source longitude and latitude alongside projected\ncoordinates for tooltips and selection.\n\nWhen a custom spatial shape must render beside `geoShape`:\n\n- Keep path generation deterministic and DOM-free.\n- Emit stable scene keys for each feature.\n- Clip only when geometry should not extend beyond the plot.\n- Emit interaction points or application overlays only where they carry honest\n  semantic anchors.\n- Keep projection and geo dependencies behind the spatial chart import.\n\nSee [Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md) and\n[Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n\n## Color, boundaries, and missing data\n\n- Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude with one direction.\n- Use a diverging scale only around a meaningful center.\n- Use threshold colors when bins have defined policy meaning.\n- Keep boundary strokes visible in light and dark themes without overpowering\n  fill.\n- Give missing, suppressed, and out-of-domain features distinct treatment.\n- Avoid a categorical palette for high-cardinality numeric values.\n\nA legend is required whenever fill or vector color carries quantitative\nmeaning.\n\n## Accessibility and interaction\n\nA map should not be the only route to critical regional values. Provide a table\nor list with the same feature names, values, units, and current selection.\n\nFor interactive maps:\n\n- Focus and selection should resolve stable feature IDs.\n- Zoom and pan state belongs to the application.\n- Reset, keyboard, and touch paths should reach equivalent extents.\n- Wheel capture should not trap page scrolling unexpectedly.\n- Tooltips should name the region or sample, value, and unit.\n\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) defines\nthe controlled gesture loop.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Validate feature joins and report unmatched IDs.\n- State projection, aggregation unit, date range, and denominator.\n- Keep color domains comparable across views and revisions.\n- Test antimeridian, empty, missing, and extremely small features where\n  applicable.\n- Bound projected point and vector counts.\n- Verify labels and legends at narrow widths.\n- Provide a non-spatial exact-value path through\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Maps and Spatial Charts\ndescription: Build choropleths, bubble maps, globes, routes, and vector fields for geographic or projected spatial questions.\n---\n\nSpatial charts encode values in a coordinate system whose geometry already has\nmeaning. A geographic map uses a projection and feature boundaries. A vector\nfield uses position, direction, and magnitude. Neither should be treated as an\nordinary categorical chart with decorative shapes.\n\n## Choose the spatial encoding\n\n| Reader question                                           | Start with                              |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| How does an aggregate differ across named regions?        | Choropleth                              |\n| Where did individual events occur?                        | Projected point layer                   |\n| How do direction and magnitude vary over a sampled plane? | Vector field                            |\n| How does a path move through space?                       | Projected line with directional context |\n| Must small regions be compared precisely by value?        | Sorted bars or a table beside the map   |\n\nThe application chooses the projection and spatial sampling policy. The\nopt-in `@tanstack/charts/geo` entry uses `d3-geo` to turn GeoJSON into shared\nscene paths and interaction points. See\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Compare regional aggregates\n\nA choropleth joins one value to each named geographic feature and maps that\nvalue through a color scale.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=102-world-choropleth height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=108-country-choropleth height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=109-us-state-choropleth height=480 -->\n\nThe join is part of data preparation. Match features through stable IDs, report\nunmatched records, and distinguish missing values from zero. Keep the color\ndomain and legend explicit so one filtered region cannot silently rescale the\nmeaning of every other fill.\n\nArea draws attention. A large region can appear important even when its value\nis ordinary. Pair the map with a sorted table or bars when precise ranking is\npart of the task.\n\n[Legends and Color](../guides/legends-and-color.md) covers sequential,\ndiverging, and threshold choices.\n\n## Prepare atlas boundaries\n\nTanStack accepts GeoJSON and does not bundle a political boundary dataset.\nConvert an application-owned TopoJSON atlas once, validate its feature count\nand IDs, then keep that geometry stable while values change.\n\n<!-- docs-example: geo-atlas-ingest typecheck -->\n\n```ts\nimport { feature } from 'topojson-client'\nimport worldAtlas from 'world-atlas/countries-110m.json'\nimport type {\n  GeometryCollection,\n  Objects,\n  Topology,\n} from 'topojson-specification'\n\ninterface AtlasProperties {\n  name: string\n}\n\ntype WorldObjects = Objects<AtlasProperties> & {\n  countries: GeometryCollection<AtlasProperties>\n}\n\nconst topology = worldAtlas as unknown as Topology<WorldObjects>\nconst countries = feature<AtlasProperties>(topology, topology.objects.countries)\n```\n\n`world-atlas` redistributes Natural Earth boundaries; `us-atlas` provides\nCensus-derived state geometry. `topojson-client.feature` performs the standard\nconversion to GeoJSON consumed by `geoShape`. These remain application\ndependencies, so neither their data nor the converter enters ordinary\nCartesian or geo-only consumer bundles.\n\n## Project GeoJSON responsively\n\nGive `geoShape` a projection factory and an explicit fit target. This\nself-contained example uses a small planar floor plan. The mark fits the\nprojection to the final plot bounds again whenever the chart resizes.\n\n```ts group=floor-plan env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { geoShape } from '@tanstack/charts/geo'\nimport { geoIdentity } from 'd3-geo'\nimport { floorPlan } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    geoShape(floorPlan.features, {\n      key: (feature) => feature.properties.id,\n      projection: {\n        type: geoIdentity,\n        fit: floorPlan,\n      },\n      fill: '#dbeafe',\n      stroke: '#2563eb',\n      strokeWidth: 1.5,\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  margin: 12,\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=floor-plan file=/src/data.ts collapsed\ninterface FloorPlanFeature {\n  type: 'Feature'\n  properties: { id: number; name: string }\n  geometry: {\n    type: 'Polygon'\n    coordinates: [number, number][][]\n  }\n}\n\ninterface FloorPlan {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection'\n  features: FloorPlanFeature[]\n}\n\nexport const floorPlan: FloorPlan = {\n  type: 'FeatureCollection',\n  features: [\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 1, name: 'Studio' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [0, 0],\n            [48, 0],\n            [48, 28],\n            [0, 28],\n            [0, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n    {\n      type: 'Feature',\n      properties: { id: 2, name: 'Office' },\n      geometry: {\n        type: 'Polygon',\n        coordinates: [\n          [\n            [52, 0],\n            [84, 0],\n            [84, 28],\n            [52, 28],\n            [52, 0],\n          ],\n        ],\n      },\n    },\n  ],\n}\n```\n\n`geoShape` uses `geoPath` for each feature and D3 centroids for focus. The\nfloor plan's source `properties.id` supplies stable identity. Supply `anchor`\nonly when a geographic feature needs a different semantic longitude/latitude\nthan its spherical centroid. The complete option contract is in\n[Geo Shape Mark](../reference/marks/geo.md).\n\n[Open the complete 121-polygon Westport House catalog example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/40-geojson-map/).\n\n## Project points by magnitude\n\nPoint and MultiPoint features use D3 `geoPath().pointRadius()`. The `r`\nchannel can carry pixels directly; `rScale` maps a quantitative value first.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=103-bubble-map height=480 -->\n\n## Change the projection\n\nThe same GeoJSON can use any D3 projection factory. Sphere and graticule\ngeometry are ordinary `geoShape` layers.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=104-orthographic-globe height=480 -->\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=110-projection-gallery height=520 -->\n\n## Layer routes over geography\n\nPolygon, LineString, and Point features can share one responsive projection.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=105-route-map height=480 -->\n\n## Show direction and magnitude\n\nA vector field places an arrow at each sampled position. Direction uses angle;\nmagnitude can use length, color, or both.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=42-vector-field height=480 -->\n\nChoose a sampling density that remains legible at the smallest container. More\narrows can obscure the flow instead of adding evidence. When pixel length\nencodes magnitude, define a bounded scale and disclose whether vectors were\nnormalized.\n\nVector channel and anchor options are listed in\n[Rules, Links, Arrows, Vectors, and Ticks](../reference/marks/rules-links-arrows-vectors-and-ticks.md).\n\n## Projection and responsive layout\n\nA projection turns geographic coordinates into planar geometry. Fit it to the\nresolved plot bounds, not the viewport, and recompute when the chart container\nchanges. Preserve source longitude and latitude alongside projected\ncoordinates for tooltips and selection.\n\nWhen a custom spatial shape must render beside `geoShape`:\n\n- Keep path generation deterministic and DOM-free.\n- Emit stable scene keys for each feature.\n- Clip only when geometry should not extend beyond the plot.\n- Emit interaction points or application overlays only where they carry honest\n  semantic anchors.\n- Keep projection and geo dependencies behind the spatial chart import.\n\nSee [Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md) and\n[Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n\n## Color, boundaries, and missing data\n\n- Use a sequential scale for ordered magnitude with one direction.\n- Use a diverging scale only around a meaningful center.\n- Use threshold colors when bins have defined policy meaning.\n- Keep boundary strokes visible in light and dark themes without overpowering\n  fill.\n- Give missing, suppressed, and out-of-domain features distinct treatment.\n- Avoid a categorical palette for high-cardinality numeric values.\n\nA legend is required whenever fill or vector color carries quantitative\nmeaning.\n\n## Accessibility and interaction\n\nA map should not be the only route to critical regional values. Provide a table\nor list with the same feature names, values, units, and current selection.\n\nFor interactive maps:\n\n- Focus and selection should resolve stable feature IDs.\n- Zoom and pan state belongs to the application.\n- Reset, keyboard, and touch paths should reach equivalent extents.\n- Wheel capture should not trap page scrolling unexpectedly.\n- Tooltips should name the region or sample, value, and unit.\n\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) defines\nthe controlled gesture loop.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Validate feature joins and report unmatched IDs.\n- State projection, aggregation unit, date range, and denominator.\n- Keep color domains comparable across views and revisions.\n- Test antimeridian, empty, missing, and extremely small features where\n  applicable.\n- Bound projected point and vector counts.\n- Verify labels and legends at narrow widths.\n- Provide a non-spatial exact-value path through\n  [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md).\n"}
